00:00:01.780 --> 00:00:05.680
We've seen the mathematical definition of the cost function.

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:10.200
Now let's build some intuition about what the cost function is really doing.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:14.680
In this video, we'll walk through one example to see how the cost function could be used

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:17.640
to find the best parameters for your model.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:21.220
I know this video is a little bit longer than the others, but bear with me, I think

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:22.660
it'll be worth it.

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:26.880
To recap, here's what we've seen about the cost function so far.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:33.400
You want to fit a straight line to the training data, so you have this model, fwB of x is w

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.800
times x plus b.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:40.040
And here, the model's parameters are w and b.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:45.340
Now depending on the values chosen for these parameters, you get different straight lines

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:47.120
like this.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:52.480
And you want to find values for w and b so that the straight line fits the training

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:54.400
data well.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:01:01.520
To measure how well a choice of w and b fits the training data, you have a cost function

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:03.280
j.

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:08.960
And what the cost function j does is it measures the difference between the model's predictions

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:13.520
and the actual true values for y.

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:18.840
What you see later is that linear regression will try to find values for w and b that

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:23.320
make j of w be as small as possible.

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:25.760
In math, we write it like this.

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:33.440
We want to minimize j as a function of w and b.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:39.240
So now, in order for us to better visualize the cost function j, let's work with a simplified

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:42.120
version of the linear regression model.

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:48.920
We're going to use the model fw of x is w times x.

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:53.880
You can think of this as taking the original model on the left and getting rid of the parameter

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:58.160
b, or setting the parameter b equal to 0.

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:01.280
So it just goes away from the equation.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:05.320
So f is now just w times x.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:11.520
So you now have just one parameter w, and your cost function j looks similar to what

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:17.680
it was before, taking the difference and squaring it, except that now, f is equal

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:25.320
to w times xi, and j is now a function of just w.

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:30.040
And the go becomes a little bit different as well, because you have just one parameter

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:32.560
w, not w and b.

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:41.880
So with this simplified model, the go is to find a value for w that minimizes j of w.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:48.640
To see this visually, what this means is that if b is set to 0, then f defines a line that

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:50.520
looks like this.

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:55.960
And you see that the line passes through the origin here, because when x is 0, well, f

00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:58.400
of x is 0 too.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:03.240
Now using this simplified model, let's see how the cost function changes as you choose

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:07.240
different values for the parameter w.

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:13.880
In particular, let's look at graphs of the model f of x and the cost function j.

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:22.240
I'm going to plot these side by side, and you'll be able to see how the two are related.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:29.280
First notice that for f subscript w, when the parameter w is fixed, that is, is always

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:35.600
a constant value, then fw is only a function of x.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:43.000
Which means that the estimated value of y depends on the value of the input x.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:51.560
In contrast, looking to the right, the cost function j is a function of w, where w controls

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:55.240
the slope of the line defined by fw.

00:03:55.240 --> 00:04:03.920
So the cost defined by j depends on a parameter, in this case, the parameter w.

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:11.760
So let's go ahead and plot these functions fw of x and j of w side by side, so you can

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:15.760
see how they are related.

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:22.600
We'll start with the model that is the function fw of x on the left.

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:30.000
Here the input feature x is on the horizontal axis, and the output value y is on the vertical

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.000
axis.

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:40.520
Here's a plot of three points representing the training set at positions 1, 1, 2, 2, and 3, 3.

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:44.960
Let's pick a value for w, say w is 1.

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:55.400
So for this choice of w, the function fw looks like this straight line with a slope of 1.

00:04:55.400 --> 00:05:03.500
Now what you can do next is calculate the cost j when w equals 1.

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:08.680
So you may recall that the cost function is defined as follows, is the squared error cost

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:10.040
function.

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:19.400
So if you substitute fw of xi with w times xi, the cost function looks like this, where

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:25.120
this expression is now w times xi minus yi.

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:30.960
So for this value of w, it turns out that the error term inside the cost function, this

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:37.760
w times xi minus yi is equal to 0 for each of the three data points.

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:42.240
Because for this data set, when x is 1, then y is 1.

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:46.960
When w is also 1, then f of x equals 1.

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:53.440
So f of x equals y for this first training example, and the difference is 0.

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:57.960
Plugging this into the cost function j, you get 0 squared.

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:04.920
Similarly, when x is 2, then y is 2, and f of x is also 2.

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:09.200
So again, f of x equals y for the second training example.

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:15.360
In the cost function, the squared error for the second example is also 0 squared.

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:22.880
Finally, when x is 3, then y is 3, and f of 3 is also 3.

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:27.280
In the cost function, the third squared error term is also 0 squared.

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:36.000
So for all three examples in this training set, f of xi equals yi for each training example

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:37.000
i.

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:43.080
So f of xi minus yi is 0.

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:52.760
So for this particular data set, when w is 1, then the cost j is equal to 0.

00:06:52.760 --> 00:06:58.280
Now what you can do on the right is plot the cost function j.

00:06:58.280 --> 00:07:04.840
And notice that because the cost function is a function of the parameter w, the horizontal

00:07:04.840 --> 00:07:15.480
axis is now labeled w and not x, and the vertical axis is now j and not y.

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:20.520
So you have j of 1 equals to 0.

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:27.080
In other words, when w equals 1, j of w is 0.

00:07:27.080 --> 00:07:30.800
So let me go ahead and plot that.

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:36.600
Now let's look at how f and j change for different values of w.

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:38.660
W can take on a range of values, right?

00:07:38.660 --> 00:07:46.040
So w can take on negative values, w can be 0, and it can take on positive values too.

00:07:46.040 --> 00:07:50.760
So what if w is equal to 0.5 instead of 1?

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:53.880
What would these graphs look like then?

00:07:53.880 --> 00:07:55.520
Let's go ahead and plot that.

00:07:55.520 --> 00:08:03.360
So let's set w to be equal to 0.5, and in this case, the function f of x now looks like

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:09.480
this, is aligned with a slope equal to 0.5.

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:16.800
And let's also compute the cost j when w is 0.5.

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:20.880
Recall that the cost function is measuring the squared error or difference between the

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:32.120
estimated value, that is y hat i, which is f of xi, and the true value, that is yi, for

00:08:32.120 --> 00:08:34.480
each example i.

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:40.520
So visually, you can see that the error or difference is equal to the height of this

00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:47.240
vertical line here when x is equal to 1, because this little line is the gap between

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:53.720
the actual value of y and the value that the function f predicted, which is a bit further

00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:55.440
down here.

00:08:55.440 --> 00:09:04.960
So for this first example, when x is 1, f of x is 0.5, so the squared error on the

00:09:04.960 --> 00:09:11.120
first example is 0.5 minus 1 squared.

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:15.880
Remember the cost function will sum over all the training examples in the training set.

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:19.640
So let's go on to the second training example.

00:09:19.640 --> 00:09:30.040
When x is 2, the model is predicting f of x is 1, and the actual value of y is 2.

00:09:30.040 --> 00:09:35.640
So the error for the second example is equal to the height of this little line segment

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:42.400
here, and the squared error is the square of the length of this line segment.

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:46.760
So you get 1 minus 2 squared.

00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:48.800
Let's do the third example.

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:58.040
Repeating this process, the error here, also shown by this line segment, is 1.5 minus 3

00:09:58.040 --> 00:09:59.040
squared.

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:06.200
Next, we sum up all of these terms, which turns out to be equal to 3.5.

00:10:06.200 --> 00:10:15.480
Then we multiply this term by 1 over 2m, where m is the number of training examples.

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:20.280
Since there are three training examples, m equals 3.

00:10:20.280 --> 00:10:29.880
So this is equal to 1 over 2 times 3, where this m here is 3.

00:10:29.880 --> 00:10:35.800
If we work out the math, this turns out to be 3.5 divided by 6.

00:10:35.800 --> 00:10:40.680
So the cost j is about 0.58.

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:45.360
Let's go ahead and plot that over there on the right.

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:48.640
Now let's try one more value for w.

00:10:48.640 --> 00:10:57.560
How about if w equals 0, what does the graph for f and j look like when w is equal to 0?

00:10:57.560 --> 00:11:04.480
It turns out that if w is equal to 0, then f of x is just this horizontal line that is

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:07.280
exactly on the x-axis.

00:11:07.280 --> 00:11:12.840
And so the error for each example is a line that goes from each point down to the horizontal

00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:17.280
line that represents f of x equals 0.

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:27.040
So the cost j when w equals 0 is 1 over 2m times the quantity 1 squared plus 2 squared

00:11:27.040 --> 00:11:36.480
plus 3 squared, and that's equal to 1 over 6 times 14, which is about 2.33.

00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:45.280
So let's plot this point where w is 0 and j of 0 is 2.33 over here.

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:48.480
And you can keep doing this for other values of w.

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:53.920
Since w can be any number, it can also be a negative value.

00:11:53.920 --> 00:12:03.080
So if w is negative 0.5, then the line f is a downward sloping line like this.

00:12:03.080 --> 00:12:09.800
It turns out that when w is negative 0.5, then you end up with an even higher cost around

00:12:09.800 --> 00:12:14.920
5.25, which is this point up here.

00:12:14.920 --> 00:12:20.360
And you can continue computing the cost function for different values of w and so on and plot

00:12:20.360 --> 00:12:22.000
these, right?

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:27.160
So it turns out that by computing a range of values, you can slowly trace out what the

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:29.760
cost function j looks like.

00:12:29.760 --> 00:12:32.620
And that's what j is.

00:12:32.620 --> 00:12:41.160
To recap, each value of parameter w corresponds to a different straight line fit, f of x

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:43.720
on the graph to the left.

00:12:43.720 --> 00:12:51.080
And for the given training set, that choice for a value of w corresponds to a single point,

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:54.240
a single point on the graph on the right.

00:12:54.240 --> 00:12:59.960
Because for each value of w, you can calculate the cost j of w.

00:12:59.960 --> 00:13:08.240
For example, when w equals 1, this corresponds to this straight line fit through the data.

00:13:08.240 --> 00:13:15.360
And it also corresponds to this point on the graph of j, where w equals 1 and the

00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:19.120
cost j of 1 equals 0.

00:13:19.160 --> 00:13:26.400
Whereas when w equals 0.5, this gives you this line which has a smaller slope.

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:32.240
And this line in combination with the training set corresponds to this point on the cost

00:13:32.240 --> 00:13:37.240
function graph at w equals 0.5.

00:13:37.240 --> 00:13:42.800
So for each value of w, you wind up with a different line and this corresponding cost

00:13:42.800 --> 00:13:44.800
j of w.

00:13:44.800 --> 00:13:49.040
And you can use these points to trace out this plot on the right.

00:13:49.040 --> 00:13:55.360
Given this, how can you choose the value of w that results in the function f fitting

00:13:55.360 --> 00:13:57.160
the data well?

00:13:57.160 --> 00:14:03.320
Well, as you can imagine, choosing a value of w that causes j of w to be as small as

00:14:03.320 --> 00:14:06.240
possible seems like a good bet.

00:14:06.240 --> 00:14:11.240
j is the cost function that measures how big the squared errors are.

00:14:11.360 --> 00:14:16.560
So choosing w that minimizes these squared errors, makes them as small as possible, would

00:14:16.560 --> 00:14:18.440
give us a good model.

00:14:18.440 --> 00:14:23.720
In this example, if you were to choose the value of w that results in the smallest possible

00:14:23.720 --> 00:14:29.320
value of j of w, you'd end up picking w equals 1.

00:14:29.320 --> 00:14:32.040
And as you can see, that's actually a pretty good choice.

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:36.960
This results in a line that fits the training data very well.

00:14:36.960 --> 00:14:42.680
So that's how in linear regression, you use the cost function to find the value of

00:14:42.680 --> 00:14:46.680
w that minimizes j.

00:14:46.680 --> 00:14:53.660
Or in the more general case, when we had parameters w and b rather than just w, you find the values

00:14:53.660 --> 00:14:58.660
of w and b that minimize j.

00:14:58.660 --> 00:15:05.900
So to summarize, you solve plots of both f and j and work through how the two are related.

00:15:05.900 --> 00:15:10.940
As you vary w or vary w and b, you end up with different straight lines.

00:15:10.940 --> 00:15:16.480
And when that straight line passes close to data, the cost j is small.

00:15:16.480 --> 00:15:22.460
So the goal of linear regression is to find the parameters w or w and b that results in

00:15:22.460 --> 00:15:26.820
the smallest possible value for the cost function j.

00:15:26.820 --> 00:15:32.100
Now in this video, we worked through our example with a simplified problem using only

00:15:32.100 --> 00:15:33.100
